# Onboarding Research & Design for a SaaS ERP (iBox)

## The Problem

iBox is a complex, multi-module ERP used by retail, wholesale, and manufacturing businesses. Onboarding depended entirely on human specialists ("catchers") who manually configured the system for each client over 2–3 days. New users couldn't start on their own: overloaded forms, unfamiliar terms, and no guidance meant most questions went straight to support.

The result: slow time-to-value, high support load, and fragile, person-dependent onboarding.

## My Role

I ran the discovery and defined the structure and logic of a self-service onboarding concept. Visual/UI design was handled by a designer, and implementation by the product and engineering teams; my contribution was the research, problem analysis, and the onboarding logic — what to ask, in what order, and which path each user type should follow. I packaged the concept into a presentation for the team.

### Research

I interviewed the onboarding specialists who configure the system for clients, and gathered knowledge from subject-matter experts across the product. From these interviews I mapped:

- How clients actually enter the product and where they get stuck
- What differs between business types (retail / wholesale / manufacturing)
- Which settings are critical to get right from day one (and can't be changed later)
- When users reach their "aha moment" — and when they abandon the product

### Problem Analysis

I documented the real friction points: overloaded forms, confusing terminology, role/access complexity, and the gap between buying a solution and reaching a result. I identified that onboarding needed to deliver a *quick first success* rather than a full business setup.

### Onboarding Logic & Structure

Based on the research, I defined a segmented, self-service onboarding concept:

- A **setup wizard** — a short set of questions (business type, language, currency, batch tracking, warehouses, staff) that configures sensible defaults and shows the right modules
- **Segmented onboarding tracks** by business type, each with a different first step (retail starts with a fast sale; wholesale with invoices; manufacturing with a tech card)
- A **progress checklist** for first actions (setup → first product → first operation → first report)
- A **final "aha" screen** reflecting the user's own data back to them ("you now have X products, Y clients — you've taken control of your business")
- **Contextual in-product hints** placed exactly where users get stuck (complex forms, roles, reports)

I also accounted for localization: Uzbek-language support was critical for trust in regional markets, and machine translation alone wasn't viable — human translation of the base content had to come first.

## What This Shows

This work goes beyond writing documentation: it's user research, problem analysis, and structuring the logic of a complex onboarding flow — turning expert-dependent, manual setup into a structured, self-service experience.
